const assertString = (v: unknown): asserts v is string => {
  if (typeof v !== "string") throw new TypeError("Expected a string");
};

function shout(x: unknown) {
  assertString(x);
  return x.toUpperCase();
}

explanation:
TypeScript only treats a call as an assertion if **every name in the call target is declared with an explicit type**. The full error on the call is: *Assertions require every name in the call target to be declared with an explicit type annotation.* (TS2775). Because the assertion isn't applied, `x` stays `unknown` and `x.toUpperCase()` also errors.

Here the arrow function has an `asserts` return type, but the variable `assertString` has no annotation: its type is inferred from the initializer, so the call doesn't count. The rule exists so control-flow analysis can spot assertion calls without first inferring types.

Either fix works:

```ts
// 1. function declaration: its signature is explicit
function assertString(v: unknown): asserts v is string {
  if (typeof v !== "string") throw new TypeError("Expected a string");
}

// 2. annotate the const with a function type
const assertString: (v: unknown) => asserts v is string = (v) => {
  if (typeof v !== "string") throw new TypeError("Expected a string");
};
```

The same rule applies to every name in a dotted call: if `helpers` is an unannotated object literal, `helpers.assertString(x)` hits TS2775 too, even when `assertString` itself is a `function` declaration.

These are compile-time errors only. At runtime the original code still throws for non-strings and works for strings; you just lose the narrowing.

