type Opts = { prefix?: string };

function label(opts: Opts, items: string[]) {
  if (opts.prefix) {
    const width = opts.prefix.length; // line A
    return items.map(i => opts.prefix.toUpperCase() + i.padStart(width)); // line B
  }
  return items;
}

explanation:
The `if (opts.prefix)` check narrows `opts.prefix` from `string | undefined` to `string`, so line A compiles. Line B is different: the arrow function is a separate function, and TypeScript doesn't track *when* a callback will run. By then, any code holding a reference to the same object could have set `opts.prefix = undefined`, so narrowing of a property access path is reset inside the callback and you get **TS18048: 'opts.prefix' is possibly 'undefined'**. (`map` happens to call it immediately, but TypeScript doesn't model that.) Marking the property `readonly` doesn't change this; in TypeScript 5.8 it still errors.

A `const` can never be reassigned, so its narrowing *is* kept inside closures:

```ts
function label(opts: Opts, items: string[]) {
  const { prefix } = opts; // or: const prefix = opts.prefix;
  if (prefix) {
    const width = prefix.length;
    return items.map(i => prefix.toUpperCase() + i.padStart(width));
  }
  return items;
}
```

Since TypeScript 5.4 the same applies to `let` variables and parameters that are not assigned anywhere after the closure is created. That improvement does not cover property paths like `opts.prefix`, which is why the original code still errors in every 5.x version.

snippets.code:
function label(v: string | number | null) {
  if (v) {
    return `Value: ${v}`;
  } else {
    v; // <- type of v here?
    return 'No value';
  }
}

explanation:
TypeScript narrows `if (v)` by checking each member of the union:

- `null` is always falsy: removed from the `if` branch, kept in the `else`.
- `string` can be truthy (`'hi'`) or falsy (`''`): kept in **both** branches.
- `number` can be truthy (`42`) or falsy (`0`, `NaN`): kept in **both** branches.

So the `if` branch is `string | number`, and the `else` branch is still `string | number | null`. TypeScript doesn't rewrite `string` as `""` there; it just keeps the whole type.

Literal types are different, because each literal is either always truthy or always falsy, so they *do* get split:

```ts
function f(v: boolean | 'a' | 0 | 1 | null) {
  if (v) {
    v; // true | 1 | "a"
  } else {
    v; // false | 0 | null
  }
}
```

The runtime trap: `label(0)` and `label('')` both return `'No value'`, even though `0` and `''` may be perfectly valid inputs. TypeScript doesn't flag this, because the code type-checks. When `0` or `''` are valid values, test for the missing case explicitly: `if (v !== null)` (or `if (v != null)` to exclude both `null` and `undefined`). In that `if` branch `v` is `string | number`, and in the `else` branch it is exactly `null`.

